al and imaging  so  i think i'm going to need my glasses for  this one  so um  this is a look at our revenue related to  insurance risk the the cmo started in i  think 1997 with 5 000 members  in 2000 we took over a contract from  what was then health insurance plan of  new york hip now emblem  which brought us about 120 000 members  and really expanded our scope  but in 2013 we now have a 140 000 lives  in full risk contracts another 78 000 in  some type of shared risk agreement  and  we are a medicaid health home contractor  so we're being paid to manage  care for another ten thousand enrollees  so 1.3 billion dollars  next year  we think that number is going to rise to  270 000 lives and about 1.8 billion  dollars which is about 50 of our  operating revenue  so that's a huge huge impact on us and  really allows us to begin  i think one of the one of the quandaries  that a lot of health systems are in now  is how do you prepare for a future of  population health management based on  quality when you're still getting paid  fee for service  getting paid fee for service doesn't  allow you really to invest in many of  the things that you need to provide  quality population health and i'll talk  about that a little bit more in a minute  so this is putting us kind of over the  marker of 50 percent where it makes  sense as a health system to to try to  move towards population health  so this is the care management  organization our cmo and  i i want to just run through some of the  programs that they run to talk about  how we do what we do  at the very top is intensive case  management this is a group of highly  trained nurses and social workers who  work with the most needy the sickest of  our sick patients often have in addition  to multiple chronic illnesses  psychosocial issues  and other problems  chronic care management  any of you that are in the health field  know about the field of disease  management well as a primary care  physician  a lot of the contact i have with disease  management from other payers is somebody  in utah or maybe india calling me to say  well you know i see on this emergency  room claim your patient has congestive  heart failure do you have this them on  this drug that people with congestive  heart failure are supposed to be on and  it turns out in fact you know the  diagnosis was wrong the patient doesn't  have congestive heart failure but that  type of disease management program isn't  really helping me take care of my sick  patients  our disease management programs are the  real deal so for example we have rns who  are certified diabetes educators  embedded in most of our primary care  sites and they do extensive education of  patients they take patients who are not  doing well with their diabetes they help  to motivate them to take more control  over their illness and they've done a  really fantastic job of helping them  improve our diabetes care they also  while they're added in building  relationships with these patients help  coordinate their care for those patients  that require work with multiple  specialists so the nurse that works with  me  might be you know gives patients her  cell phone she might have 10 calls over  the course of two or three days for a  patient where we're starting the patient  on insulin and they need rapid  adjustments based on what their blood  glucose is that kind of capability just  doesn't exist in in our system before  and in most health systems we also have  disease management for respiratory  disease asthma  mostly telephonic but also we have an  asthma center where patients get  extensively educated about their illness  they see a pulmonologist and allergist  get a plan of care and go back to their  primary care provider as well as a  congestive heart failure program  with telemonitoring in the home so that  as you may know people with congestive  heart failure their heart is not pumping  adequately they begin to build up fluids  in their body eventually they get lots  of edema in their legs they can't  breathe  the the minute somebody starts to tip  over and gain that extra weight maybe  they didn't take their medicine maybe  they had a high salt meal we're now able  to detect that either counsel them over  the phone or bring the patient into the  physician's office right away otherwise  three days later that would have been a  ten thousand dollar hospital admission  as their heart failure progressed  uh  zach mentioned palliative care we have a  good palliative care program at  montefiore and i think the you know the  real issue here is  not just  the fact that we're we're able to give  people better plans it's that we have  folks with enough time and enough  expertise to really talk with people  about how to plan what is going to be  the end of their life we're not doing  this with patients who have cancer that  can be easily treated these are p  patients with incurable illness where  the reflex of the health system and for  families that don't have support is you  call 9-1-1 when things start looking bad  and they're back in the hospital so this  allows people to have much better  symptom management and to be able to  create goals of care and a plan for how  to achieve them  whether that's hospice at home or other  ways  to to have a better quality of life at  the end  we have a managed behavioral care  program and we're beginning to  experiment now with integrated  behavioral care in primary care so again  you know our mantra for  most of my career has been if you can't  bill for it we can't do it because we  can't afford to do it in the office  so now we have  psychiatrists  in our offices or even connected  telephonically and through our  electronic medical record who will  consult with a social worker work with a  primary care physician send suggestions  around medication management that's not  a billable service so they you can't  afford to do it in the regular world  here where we're dealing with  essentially a 