ercent  reduction in readmissions in our group  and in 2012  one of the things that zach didn't  mention is that in an aco you only get  to share the savings if you hit quality  measures if you don't hit the quality  measures there's no savings return back  to the aco so in 2012 with the  requirement was only that you could  report all of the quality measures we  were able to do that and we're working  very hard now at a number of plans to  improve and make sure that we hit the  targets for next year  thank you john right great  well i think i think that's a great  opening for  i've gotten a lot of questions from the  prior  panels about costs that i'm going to ask  but i think that's a good opening for  talking about  zach this  fundamental point that you brought up  which might be summed up to uh uh  with the um phrase the prices are too  damn high  and that is that the us obviously has  extraordinarily high price levels  compared with uh other countries you  know even with medicare rates being  relatively high they're much lower than  most private insurance plans are paying  we've seen consolidation on the provider  side um so what is the  what is the um what is the prescription  that you would offer and obviously you  said we should be moving away from  paying fee for service well what what's  a path by which we could get there uh  how quickly could we travel it and how  much could it take place through the  work of ipab and maybe you can explain  what ipab is to the group sure so it's a  great question so essentially to distill  it down the price for hip replacement in  the united states is three times with  the prices for hip replacement in  virtually every other country and it's  the same thing we're not we're not doing  anything mythically different why is it  well some of it is that we've seen a lot  of mergers so  we're guilty of it yale new haven's  buying up everything in the surrounding  areas  and and when you have entities merge and  markets become mo